{"slug": "pulse-width-modulation-duty-cycle", "targets": ["pulse width modulation", "pwm duty cycle"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">PWM Duty Cycle Table: Pulse Width Modulation at a Glance</h2><p><strong>Pulse width modulation (PWM)</strong> controls average power by switching a supply fully ON and OFF very fast and changing how long it stays ON in each period. The PWM duty cycle is that ON time as a percentage of the period: D = (Ton \u00f7 T) \u00d7 100.</p><p>For a 12 V supply, the average output is simply V avg = D \u00d7 12 V:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">PWM duty cycle</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Ton in a 1 ms period (1 kHz)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Average output from 12 V</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 %</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.1 ms</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1.2 V</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">25 %</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.25 ms</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3 V</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">50 %</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.5 ms</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">6 V</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">75 %</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.75 ms</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">9 V</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 %</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 ms</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">12 V</td></tr></table></div><p>Changing the duty cycle changes the average voltage, while the frequency stays fixed. That is why the same PWM signal can dim an LED, set a DC motor speed or drive a servo.</p>"}